 "questions": [
  {
   "xml_index": 1,
   "xml_name": "L04_001_Define And Call A Function",
   "display_name": "Define And Call A Function",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Welcome to Lab 4! So far, every program you've written has been one flat sequence of steps. Now we'll learn <b>functions</b> - named, reusable blocks of code that you define once and can run (\"call\") whenever you need them.</p>\n<p>A function is defined with the <code>def</code> keyword, a name, parentheses, and a colon. Everything indented underneath is the function's <b>body</b> - the code that runs each time the function is called.</p>\n<pre>def say_hi():\n    print(\"Hi there!\")\n\nsay_hi()</pre>\n<p><code>def say_hi():</code> just <b>defines</b> the function - it does <b>not</b> run the body yet! The body only runs when we <b>call</b> the function later, by writing its name followed by parentheses: <code>say_hi()</code>. This prints <code>Hi there!</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> exactly like <code>if</code> and loops, the function body must be indented with <b>Tab</b> (or 4 spaces), so Python knows which lines belong inside the function.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a function named <code>greet</code> that takes no arguments and prints exactly <code>Hello</code>. Then call it once.</p>\n<p><b>Expected Output:</b></p>\n<pre>Hello</pre>",
   "answerpreload": "def greet():\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# greet()",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "greet()",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "Hello",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 2,
   "xml_name": "L04_002_Call A Function Multiple Times",
   "display_name": "Call A Function Multiple Times",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>The whole point of a function is that once it's defined, you can call it as many times as you like, without rewriting its body.</p>\n<pre>def say_hi():\n    print(\"Hi there!\")\n\nsay_hi()\nsay_hi()\nsay_hi()</pre>\n<p>This prints <code>Hi there!</code> three times - once for each call - even though we only wrote the body once.</p>\n<p><strong>Your task:</strong> Define a function named <code>greet</code> that prints exactly <code>Hello</code>. Then call it exactly <strong>three</strong> times.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected Output:</strong></p>\n<pre>Hello\nHello\nHello</pre>",
   "answerpreload": "def greet():\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space below to call your function 3 times ----\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "Hello\nHello\nHello",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 3,
   "xml_name": "L04_003_Function With A Parameter",
   "display_name": "With A Parameter",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Functions become much more useful when they can take <b>input</b>, in the form of <b>parameters</b> - variables listed inside the parentheses, which receive whatever values are passed in when the function is called.</p>\n<pre>def greet(name):\n    print(\"Hello, \" + name + \"!\")\n\ngreet(\"Alice\")\ngreet(\"Bob\")</pre>\n<p>Here, <code>name</code> is a parameter. When we call <code>greet(\"Alice\")</code>, the text <code>\"Alice\"</code> is passed in and becomes the value of <code>name</code> inside the function body for that call. Calling <code>greet(\"Bob\")</code> right after uses a completely different value of <code>name</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a function named <code>greet</code> that takes one parameter <code>name</code> and prints <code>Hello, &lt;name&gt;!</code>. Then call it once with the argument <code>\"Alice\"</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Expected Output:</b></p>\n<pre>Hello, Alice!</pre>",
   "answerpreload": "def greet(name):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# greet(\"Alice\")",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "greet(\"Alice\")",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "Hello, Alice!",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 4,
   "xml_name": "L04_004_Call Function With Different Arguments",
   "display_name": "Call Function With Different Arguments",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's call the same function several times, passing a <b>different</b> argument each time - this is exactly why parameters exist.</p>\n<pre>def greet(name):\n    print(\"Hello, \" + name + \"!\")\n\ngreet(\"Alice\")\ngreet(\"Bob\")\ngreet(\"Charlie\")</pre>\n<p>Each call uses the same function body, but <code>name</code> takes on a different value each time - producing three different greetings from one function definition.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a function named <code>greet</code> that takes one parameter <code>name</code> and prints <code>Hello, &lt;name&gt;!</code>. Then read three names (one per line) and call <code>greet</code> once for each.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>Alice\nBob\nCharlie</pre> Output: <pre>Hello, Alice!\nHello, Bob!\nHello, Charlie!</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "def greet(name):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# greet(\"Alice\")",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "n1 = input()\nn2 = input()\nn3 = input()\ngreet(n1)\ngreet(n2)\ngreet(n3)",
     "stdin": "Alice\nBob\nCharlie",
     "expected": "Hello, Alice!\nHello, Bob!\nHello, Charlie!",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "n1 = input()\nn2 = input()\nn3 = input()\ngreet(n1)\ngreet(n2)\ngreet(n3)",
     "stdin": "Zoe\nSam\nMax",
     "expected": "Hello, Zoe!\nHello, Sam!\nHello, Max!",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
