  {
   "xml_index": 5,
   "xml_name": "L04_005_Function That Returns A Value",
   "display_name": "That Returns A Value",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>So far our functions have only <code>print()</code>ed things directly. Often, we instead want a function to <b>compute</b> a value and hand it back to whoever called it - this is what <code>return</code> is for.</p>\n<pre>def square_it(a):\n    return a * a\n\nresult = square_it(5)\nprint(result)</pre>\n<p><code>return a * a</code> sends the computed value <b>back</b> to the caller. Unlike <code>print()</code>, which just displays something and is done, <code>return</code> makes the function call itself <i>become</i> that value - which is why we can store it in a variable (<code>result</code>) and use it later, like any other value.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a function named <code>square_it</code> that takes one parameter <code>a</code> and <b>returns</b> its square (not print it). Then call <code>square_it(5)</code>, store the result in a variable, and print that variable.</p>\n<p><b>Expected Output:</b></p>\n<pre>25</pre>",
   "answerpreload": "def square_it(a):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(square_it(5))",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(square_it(5))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "25",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 6,
   "xml_name": "L04_006_Function With Two Parameters Returning Sum",
   "display_name": "With Two Parameters Returning Sum",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Functions can take more than one parameter, separated by commas. Let's write one that returns the sum of two numbers.</p>\n<pre>def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nprint(add(3, 4))</pre>\n<p>This prints <code>7</code>. Notice the order matters when calling: <code>add(3, 4)</code> passes <code>3</code> as <code>a</code> and <code>4</code> as <code>b</code>, matching the order they were listed in the <code>def</code> line.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a function named <code>add</code> that takes two parameters <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> and returns their sum. Then read two whole numbers (one per line), call <code>add</code> with them, and print the result.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>3\n4</pre> Output: <pre>7</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "def add(a, b):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(add(3, 4))",
   "mark": 1.0,
