  {
   "xml_index": 8,
   "xml_name": "L04_008_Function Returning Based On If Else",
   "display_name": "Returning Based On If Else",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A function's body can contain anything you've already learned, including <code>if</code>-<code>else</code>. This lets a function's return value depend on a condition.</p>\n<pre>def parity(n):\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        return \"Even\"\n    else:\n        return \"Odd\"\n\nprint(parity(7))</pre>\n<p>Notice there are <b>two</b> <code>return</code> statements here - one inside each branch. Only one of them actually runs for any given call, depending on which branch the condition takes. As soon as a <code>return</code> runs, the function immediately hands back that value and stops - it never reaches the other branch.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a function named <code>parity</code> that takes one parameter <code>n</code> and returns the string <code>\"Even\"</code> if <code>n</code> is even, or <code>\"Odd\"</code> if it's odd. Read a whole number, call <code>parity</code>, and print the result.</p>\n<p><b>Example 1:</b> Input: <pre>7</pre> Output: <pre>Odd</pre></p>\n<p><b>Example 2:</b> Input: <pre>4</pre> Output: <pre>Even</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "def parity(n):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(parity(7))",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "n = int(input())\nprint(parity(n))",
     "stdin": "7",
     "expected": "Odd",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "n = int(input())\nprint(parity(n))",
     "stdin": "4",
     "expected": "Even",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
