    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 9,
   "xml_name": "L04_009_Function Using A While Loop Inside",
   "display_name": "Using A While Loop Inside",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A function's body can also contain a loop - everything you've learned about loops works exactly the same way inside a function.</p>\n<pre>def sum_upto(n):\n    total = 0\n    i = 1\n    while i <= n:\n        total = total + i\n        i = i + 1\n    return total\n\nprint(sum_upto(5))</pre>\n<p>This is the exact same <code>while</code>-loop accumulator pattern from Lab 3, just wrapped in a function so it can be reused and given a meaningful name. Notice the <code>return total</code> comes <b>after</b> the loop has fully finished - we want the final total, not an intermediate one.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a function named <code>sum_upto</code> that takes one parameter <code>n</code> and returns the sum of all whole numbers from 1 to <code>n</code> (inclusive), using a <code>while</code> loop. Read a whole number, call <code>sum_upto</code>, and print the result.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>5</pre> Output: <pre>15</pre></p>\n<p><b>Edge case:</b> Input: <pre>1</pre> Output: <pre>1</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "def sum_upto(n):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(sum_upto(5))",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "n = int(input())\nprint(sum_upto(n))",
     "stdin": "5",
     "expected": "15",
