  {
   "xml_index": 10,
   "xml_name": "L04_010_Function Using A For Loop Inside",
   "display_name": "Using A For Loop Inside",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's do the same thing with a <code>for</code> loop instead, writing a function that computes a factorial.</p>\n<pre>def factorial(n):\n    result = 1\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        result = result * i\n    return result\n\nprint(factorial(4))</pre>\n<p>This is the same factorial pattern from Lab 3, now wrapped in a reusable function.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a function named <code>factorial</code> that takes one parameter <code>n</code> and returns <code>n!</code> (n factorial), using a <code>for</code> loop. Read a whole number, call <code>factorial</code>, and print the result.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>4</pre> Output: <pre>24</pre></p>\n<p><b>Edge case (0! is 1):</b> Input: <pre>0</pre> Output: <pre>1</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "def factorial(n):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(factorial(4))",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "n = int(input())\nprint(factorial(n))",
     "stdin": "4",
     "expected": "24",
     "extra": "",
