   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 11,
   "xml_name": "L04_011_Two Independent Functions",
   "display_name": "Two Independent Functions",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A program can define as many functions as it needs. They don't need to know about each other unless you explicitly make one call the other.</p>\n<pre>def square_it(a):\n    return a * a\n\ndef cube_it(a):\n    return a * a * a\n\nprint(square_it(3))\nprint(cube_it(3))</pre>\n<p>Here we define two completely separate functions, and call each of them independently.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define two functions: <code>square_it(a)</code> returning <code>a squared</code>, and <code>cube_it(a)</code> returning <code>a cubed</code>. Read a whole number <code>n</code>, then print <code>square_it(n)</code> followed by <code>cube_it(n)</code>, each on its own line.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>3</pre> Output: <pre>9\n27</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "def square_it(a):\n    # write your code here\n\n\ndef cube_it(a):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your functions; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(square_it(3))\n# print(cube_it(3))",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
