   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 12,
   "xml_name": "L04_012_Function Calling Another Function",
   "display_name": "Calling Another Function",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Here's something new: one function's body can call <b>another</b> function, exactly the same way your main code calls functions.</p>\n<pre>def square_it(a):\n    return a * a\n\ndef sum_of_squares(a, b):\n    return square_it(a) + square_it(b)\n\nprint(sum_of_squares(3, 4))</pre>\n<p>Inside <code>sum_of_squares</code>, we call <code>square_it</code> twice, once for each parameter, and add the two results together. As far as <code>sum_of_squares</code> is concerned, <code>square_it</code> is just another tool it can use - it doesn't matter that <code>square_it</code> happens to be a function we wrote ourselves rather than a built-in one.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define <code>square_it(a)</code> returning <code>a squared</code>, then define <code>sum_of_squares(a, b)</code> which calls <code>square_it</code> internally and returns the sum of the two squares. Read two whole numbers (one per line), call <code>sum_of_squares</code>, and print the result.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>3\n4</pre> Output: <pre>25</pre></p>\n<p>(3 squared is 9, 4 squared is 16, sum is 25)</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def square_it(a):\n    # write your code here\n\n\ndef sum_of_squares(a, b):\n    # write your code here (call square_it inside this function)\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your functions; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(sum_of_squares(3, 4))",
