  {
   "xml_index": 13,
   "xml_name": "L04_013_Compute F Of G Of X",
   "display_name": "Compute F Of G Of X",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's practice <b>composing</b> two functions explicitly from the caller's side: taking the output of one function and feeding it directly as the input to another - often written mathematically as <code>f(g(x))</code>.</p>\n<pre>def double_it(a):\n    return a * 2\n\ndef add_ten(a):\n    return a + 10\n\nx = 5\nresult = add_ten(double_it(x))\nprint(result)</pre>\n<p>Here, <code>double_it(x)</code> runs <b>first</b> (Python always evaluates the innermost parentheses first), producing <code>10</code>. That result is then passed straight into <code>add_ten(...)</code>, giving <code>20</code>. We never needed to store the intermediate result in its own variable - though we could have, for clarity.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define <code>double_it(a)</code> returning <code>a * 2</code>, and <code>add_ten(a)</code> returning <code>a + 10</code>. Read a whole number <code>x</code> and print <code>add_ten(double_it(x))</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>5</pre> Output: <pre>20</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "def double_it(a):\n    # write your code here\n\n\ndef add_ten(a):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your functions; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(add_ten(double_it(5)))",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "x = int(input())\nprint(add_ten(double_it(x)))",
