   "xml_index": 15,
   "xml_name": "L04_015_Reusing A Function Twice Inside Another",
   "display_name": "Reusing A Function Twice Inside Another",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's write another function that reuses a helper twice, just like <code>sum_of_squares</code> did earlier - but this time, using the shared helper's result to compute something new.</p>\n<pre>def square_it(a):\n    return a * a\n\ndef distance_squared(x1, y1, x2, y2):\n    dx = x2 - x1\n    dy = y2 - y1\n    return square_it(dx) + square_it(dy)\n\nprint(distance_squared(1, 1, 4, 5))</pre>\n<p>This computes the squared distance between two points, reusing <code>square_it</code> for both the horizontal and vertical differences.</p>\n<p><b>Trace through:</b> <code>dx = 4 - 1 = 3</code>, <code>dy = 5 - 1 = 4</code>. <code>square_it(3) + square_it(4) = 9 + 16 = 25</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define <code>square_it(a)</code> returning <code>a squared</code>, and <code>distance_squared(x1, y1, x2, y2)</code> which uses <code>square_it</code> to compute the squared distance between points <code>(x1, y1)</code> and <code>(x2, y2)</code>, as shown above. Read four whole numbers <code>x1, y1, x2, y2</code> (one per line), call <code>distance_squared</code>, and print the result.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>1\n1\n4\n5</pre> Output: <pre>25</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "def square_it(a):\n    # write your code here\n\n\ndef distance_squared(x1, y1, x2, y2):\n    # write your code here (call square_it inside this function)\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your functions; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(distance_squared(1, 1, 4, 5))",
