   "xml_name": "L04_016_Helper Function For Area",
   "display_name": "Helper Function For Area",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's use a very simple helper function inside a more meaningful one, showing how small reusable pieces can build up bigger calculations.</p>\n<pre>def multiply(a, b):\n    return a * b\n\ndef rectangle_area(length, breadth):\n    return multiply(length, breadth)\n\nprint(rectangle_area(5, 3))</pre>\n<p>This is a bit of a toy example (you could just write <code>length * breadth</code> directly!), but it demonstrates the idea: <code>rectangle_area</code> doesn't need to know <i>how</i> multiplication works - it just delegates that step to <code>multiply</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define <code>multiply(a, b)</code> returning <code>a * b</code>, and <code>rectangle_area(length, breadth)</code> which calls <code>multiply</code> to compute and return the area. Read two whole numbers (length, then breadth), call <code>rectangle_area</code>, and print the result.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>5\n3</pre> Output: <pre>15</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "def multiply(a, b):\n    # write your code here\n\n\ndef rectangle_area(length, breadth):\n    # write your code here (call multiply inside this function)\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your functions; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(rectangle_area(5, 3))",
