  {
   "xml_index": 20,
   "xml_name": "L04_020_Function Returning Boolean Used In Caller If",
   "display_name": "Returning Boolean Used In Caller If",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A function can return <code>True</code> or <code>False</code> directly - Python's own boolean values, the same kind a comparison like <code>n &gt; 0</code> produces. The caller can then use that returned value directly inside an <code>if</code>.</p>\n<pre>def is_prime_candidate(n):\n    return n > 1 and n % 2 != 0\n\nx = 7\nif is_prime_candidate(x):\n    print(\"Might be prime\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Definitely not prime\")</pre>\n<p>Notice <code>is_prime_candidate</code> doesn't need its own <code>if</code>-<code>else</code> at all here - the condition <code>n &gt; 1 and n % 2 != 0</code> already evaluates to <code>True</code> or <code>False</code>, so we can just <code>return</code> it directly. The caller then uses that True/False value exactly like any other condition.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a function named <code>is_prime_candidate</code> that takes one parameter <code>n</code> and returns <code>True</code> if <code>n</code> is greater than 1 <b>and</b> odd, or <code>False</code> otherwise. Read a whole number <code>x</code>; if <code>is_prime_candidate(x)</code> is True, print <code>Might be prime</code>, else print <code>Definitely not prime</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Example 1:</b> Input: <pre>7</pre> Output: <pre>Might be prime</pre></p>\n<p><b>Example 2 (edge case - fails the &gt;1 check):</b> Input: <pre>1</pre> Output: <pre>Definitely not prime</pre></p>",
