  {
   "xml_index": 21,
   "xml_name": "L04_021_Multiple Return Values Quotient And Remainder",
   "display_name": "Multiple Return Values Quotient And Remainder",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A function isn't limited to returning just one value! Python lets you <code>return</code> several values at once, separated by commas, and the caller can \"unpack\" them into several variables in a single line.</p>\n<pre>def divide(a, b):\n    q = a // b\n    r = a % b\n    return q, r\n\nquotient, remainder = divide(17, 5)\nprint(quotient)\nprint(remainder)</pre>\n<p><code>return q, r</code> packages both values together. On the caller's side, <code>quotient, remainder = divide(17, 5)</code> unpacks them straight into two separate variables, in the same order they were returned.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a function named <code>divide</code> that takes two parameters <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>, and returns both the quotient (<code>a // b</code>) and the remainder (<code>a % b</code>). Read two whole numbers, call <code>divide</code>, unpack the results into two variables, and print the quotient then the remainder, each on its own line.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>17\n5</pre> Output: <pre>3\n2</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "def divide(a, b):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# q, r = divide(17, 5)\n# print(q, r)",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
