   "xml_name": "L04_024_Multiple Return Values Three Stats",
   "display_name": "Multiple Return Values Three Stats",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>You can return more than two values too - just separate them all with commas, and unpack the same number of variables on the caller's side.</p>\n<p><b>Reminder:</b> since the average involves division, round it with <code>round(value, 2)</code> before returning it, to avoid floating-point mismatches.</p>\n<pre>def stats(a, b, c):\n    total = a + b + c\n    average = round(total / 3, 2)\n    if a >= b and a >= c:\n        largest = a\n    elif b >= c:\n        largest = b\n    else:\n        largest = c\n    return total, average, largest\n\nt, avg, m = stats(3, 7, 5)\nprint(t)\nprint(avg)\nprint(m)</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a function named <code>stats</code> that takes three whole numbers <code>a</code>, <code>b</code>, <code>c</code> and returns their sum, their average (rounded to 2 decimal places), and the largest of the three. Read three whole numbers (one per line), call <code>stats</code>, unpack the three results, and print each on its own line (sum, then average, then largest).</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>3\n7\n5</pre> Output: <pre>15\n5.0\n7</pre></p>",
