   "xml_name": "L04_025_Chaining Multiple Return Values",
   "display_name": "Chaining Multiple Return Values",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's use a multiple-return function's unpacked results as arguments to <b>another</b> function - combining everything you've learned about composition and multiple returns.</p>\n<pre>def divide(a, b):\n    return a // b, a % b\n\ndef describe(q, r):\n    if r == 0:\n        return \"Divides evenly, quotient \" + str(q)\n    else:\n        return \"Does not divide evenly, remainder \" + str(r)\n\nq, r = divide(20, 4)\nprint(describe(q, r))</pre>\n<p>We first unpack <code>divide</code>'s two return values into <code>q</code> and <code>r</code>, then pass <b>both</b> of them as separate arguments into <code>describe</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define <code>divide(a, b)</code> and <code>describe(q, r)</code> exactly as shown above. Read two whole numbers, call <code>divide</code>, unpack the results, then call <code>describe</code> with them and print its return value.</p>\n<p><b>Example 1:</b> Input: <pre>20\n4</pre> Output: <pre>Divides evenly, quotient 5</pre></p>\n<p><b>Example 2:</b> Input: <pre>17\n5</pre> Output: <pre>Does not divide evenly, remainder 2</pre></p>",
