  {
   "xml_index": 35,
   "xml_name": "L04_035_Recursion Sum Of Digits",
   "display_name": "Recursion Sum Of Digits",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Every function you've written so far has called <i>other</i> functions, or none at all. Now let's meet <b>recursion</b>: a function that calls <b>itself</b>.</p>\n<p>Every recursive function needs two parts: a <b>base case</b> (a simple situation where the function can answer directly, with no further recursive call - this is what stops the recursion from running forever), and a <b>recursive case</b> (where the function calls itself on a \"smaller\" version of the problem, and combines that result with something to get the final answer).</p>\n<pre>def sum_of_digits(n):\n    if n < 10:\n        return n\n    else:\n        return n % 10 + sum_of_digits(n // 10)\n\nprint(sum_of_digits(1234))</pre>\n<p><b>Base case:</b> if <code>n</code> is already a single digit (less than 10), its digit sum is just itself - no need to recurse further.</p>\n<p><b>Recursive case:</b> otherwise, take the last digit (<code>n % 10</code>) and add it to the digit sum of everything <i>except</i> the last digit (<code>sum_of_digits(n // 10)</code>) - a smaller version of the same problem.</p>\n<p><b>Trace through (n=1234):</b> <code>1234</code> is not &lt;10, so we compute <code>4 + sum_of_digits(123)</code>. <code>123</code> is not &lt;10, so that's <code>3 + sum_of_digits(12)</code>. <code>12</code> is not &lt;10, so that's <code>2 + sum_of_digits(1)</code>. <code>1</code> <b>is</b> &lt;10 - base case! - returns <code>1</code> directly. Unwinding: <code>2+1=3</code>, then <code>3+3=6</code>, then <code>4+6=10</code>. Final answer: <code>10</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a recursive function named <code>sum_of_digits</code> that takes one parameter <code>n</code> (a non-negative whole number) and returns the sum of its digits, using the technique shown above. Read a whole number, call <code>sum_of_digits</code>, and print the result.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>1234</pre> Output: <pre>10</pre></p>\n<p><b>Edge case (single digit - base case only):</b> Input: <pre>7</pre> Output: <pre>7</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define a recursive function named sum_of_digits that takes one int parameter: n\n# It should return the sum of n's digits. Remember: it needs a base case and a recursive case.\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(sum_of_digits(1234))",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "n = int(input())\nprint(sum_of_digits(n))",
     "stdin": "1234",
     "expected": "10",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "n = int(input())\nprint(sum_of_digits(n))",
     "stdin": "7",
     "expected": "7",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "n = int(input())\nprint(sum_of_digits(n))",
     "stdin": "999",
     "expected": "27",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "n = int(input())\nprint(sum_of_digits(n))",
     "stdin": "50",
     "expected": "5",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "n = int(input())\nprint(sum_of_digits(n))",
     "stdin": "80507",
     "expected": "20",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 36,
   "xml_name": "L04_036_Recursion Fibonacci",
   "display_name": "Recursion Fibonacci",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's write a classic recursive function: computing the <code>n</code>-th Fibonacci number, where each term is the sum of the two before it (starting 0, 1).</p>\n<pre>def fib(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    elif n == 1:\n        return 1\n    else:\n        return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\n\nprint(fib(6))</pre>\n<p><b>Base cases:</b> <code>fib(0)</code> is defined as <code>0</code>, and <code>fib(1)</code> is defined as <code>1</code> - both answered directly, no recursion needed.</p>\n<p><b>Recursive case:</b> for any <code>n</code> above 1, <code>fib(n)</code> is the sum of the two <b>previous</b> Fibonacci numbers - which we compute by calling <code>fib</code> on <code>n-1</code> and <code>n-2</code>, and adding the results. Notice this function calls itself <b>twice</b> per recursive call!</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a recursive function named <code>fib</code> that takes one parameter <code>n</code> (0 or above) and returns the <code>n</code>-th Fibonacci number (0-indexed: fib(0)=0, fib(1)=1), using the technique shown above. Read a whole number, call <code>fib</code>, and print the result.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>6</pre> Output: <pre>8</pre></p>\n<p><b>Edge case (base case, n=0):</b> Input: <pre>0</pre> Output: <pre>0</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define a recursive function named fib that takes one int parameter: n\n# It should return the n-th Fibonacci number (0-indexed: fib(0)=0, fib(1)=1)\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(fib(6))",
   "mark": 1.0,
