  {
   "xml_index": 37,
   "xml_name": "L04_037_Recursion Factorial",
   "display_name": "Recursion Factorial",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's write the factorial function recursively, and compare it in your mind to the iterative version you wrote back in Lab 4's very first questions.</p>\n<pre>def factorial(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n    else:\n        return n * factorial(n - 1)\n\nprint(factorial(4))</pre>\n<p><b>Base case:</b> <code>factorial(0)</code> is defined as <code>1</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Recursive case:</b> for <code>n</code> above 0, <code>factorial(n)</code> is <code>n</code> multiplied by <code>factorial(n - 1)</code> - a smaller version of the same problem.</p>\n<p><b>Trace through (n=4):</b> <code>factorial(4) = 4 * factorial(3)</code>, which needs <code>factorial(3) = 3 * factorial(2)</code>, which needs <code>factorial(2) = 2 * factorial(1)</code>, which needs <code>factorial(1) = 1 * factorial(0)</code>, and <code>factorial(0) = 1</code> (base case). Unwinding: <code>1*1=1</code>, <code>2*1=2</code>, <code>3*2=6</code>, <code>4*6=24</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a recursive function named <code>factorial</code> that takes one parameter <code>n</code> (0 or above) and returns <code>n!</code>, using the technique shown above. Read a whole number, call <code>factorial</code>, and print the result.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>4</pre> Output: <pre>24</pre></p>\n<p><b>Edge case (base case):</b> Input: <pre>0</pre> Output: <pre>1</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define a recursive function named factorial that takes one int parameter: n\n# It should return n! (n factorial)\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(factorial(4))",
