  {
   "xml_index": 38,
   "xml_name": "L04_038_Recursion Power",
   "display_name": "Recursion Power",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's write a recursive function to compute <code>base</code> raised to a whole-number <code>exp</code>onent, without using Python's built-in <code>**</code> operator.</p>\n<pre>def power(base, exp):\n    if exp == 0:\n        return 1\n    else:\n        return base * power(base, exp - 1)\n\nprint(power(2, 5))</pre>\n<p><b>Base case:</b> anything raised to the power 0 is <code>1</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Recursive case:</b> <code>base</code> raised to <code>exp</code> is <code>base</code> multiplied by <code>base</code> raised to <code>exp - 1</code> - each recursive call reduces the exponent by 1, moving closer to the base case.</p>\n<p><b>Trace through (base=2, exp=3):</b> <code>power(2,3) = 2 * power(2,2) = 2 * (2 * power(2,1)) = 2 * (2 * (2 * power(2,0))) = 2 * (2 * (2 * 1)) = 8</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a recursive function named <code>power</code> that takes <code>base</code> and <code>exp</code> (exp is 0 or above) and returns <code>base</code> raised to the power <code>exp</code>, using the technique shown above. Read two whole numbers <code>base</code> and <code>exp</code>, call <code>power</code>, and print the result.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>2\n5</pre> Output: <pre>32</pre></p>\n<p><b>Edge case (exponent 0):</b> Input: <pre>7\n0</pre> Output: <pre>1</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define a recursive function named power that takes two int parameters: base, exp\n# It should return base raised to the power exp, without using **\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(power(2, 5))",
