  {
   "xml_index": 39,
   "xml_name": "L04_039_Recursion Count Digits",
   "display_name": "Recursion Count Digits",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's write the digit-counting function from Lab3_part2 recursively, instead of with a <code>while</code> loop.</p>\n<pre>def count_digits(n):\n    if n < 10:\n        return 1\n    else:\n        return 1 + count_digits(n // 10)\n\nprint(count_digits(305))</pre>\n<p><b>Base case:</b> a single-digit number (less than 10) has exactly 1 digit.</p>\n<p><b>Recursive case:</b> otherwise, the digit count is <code>1</code> (for the last digit we're \"using up\") plus the digit count of everything remaining after removing the last digit (<code>n // 10</code>).</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a recursive function named <code>count_digits</code> that takes one parameter <code>n</code> (a positive whole number) and returns how many digits it has, using the technique shown above. Read a whole number, call <code>count_digits</code>, and print the result.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>305</pre> Output: <pre>3</pre></p>\n<p><b>Edge case (single digit - base case only):</b> Input: <pre>7</pre> Output: <pre>1</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define a recursive function named count_digits that takes one int parameter: n\n# It should return how many digits n has\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(count_digits(305))",
