   "xml_name": "L04_040_Capstone Sum Of Squares Of Digits",
   "display_name": "Capstone Sum Of Squares Of Digits",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>For our final question, let's combine everything from this lab: a recursive helper function, called from inside another function, to compute something new - the sum of the <b>squares</b> of a number's digits.</p>\n<pre>def digit_sum_of_squares(n):\n    if n < 10:\n        return n * n\n    else:\n        return (n % 10) * (n % 10) + digit_sum_of_squares(n // 10)\n\nprint(digit_sum_of_squares(123))</pre>\n<p>This follows the exact same recursive pattern as <code>sum_of_digits</code> from earlier in this lab, except each digit is <b>squared</b> before being added in, both in the base case and the recursive case.</p>\n<p><b>Trace through (n=123):</b> <code>123</code>: <code>3*3=9</code> plus <code>digit_sum_of_squares(12)</code>. <code>12</code>: <code>2*2=4</code> plus <code>digit_sum_of_squares(1)</code>. <code>1</code> is &lt;10 (base case): returns <code>1*1=1</code>. Unwinding: <code>4+1=5</code>, then <code>9+5=14</code>. Final answer: <code>14</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a recursive function named <code>digit_sum_of_squares</code> that takes one parameter <code>n</code> (a positive whole number) and returns the sum of the squares of its digits, using the technique shown above. Read a whole number, call <code>digit_sum_of_squares</code>, and print the result.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>123</pre> Output: <pre>14</pre></p>\n<p>(1*1 + 2*2 + 3*3 = 1 + 4 + 9 = 14)</p>\n<p><b>Edge case (single digit):</b> Input: <pre>7</pre> Output: <pre>49</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define a recursive function named digit_sum_of_squares that takes one int parameter: n\n# It should return the sum of the squares of n's digits\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(digit_sum_of_squares(123))",
